  "title": "BCIT Mind Wandering",
  "description": "EEG dataset from a long-duration simulated driving study in which participants drove in a visually complex environment with lateral vehicle perturbations, a vigilance task (detecting police vehicles), and different background audio conditions (task-relevant, non-task-relevant, internal focus). The study aimed to identify markers of driver fatigue and mind wandering during prolonged time-on-task, in comparison with a non-fatigued calibration driving session. Data include 64-channel BioSemi EEG, eye tracking, and vehicle logs.",
  "methods_description": "Participants completed a practice session, then a 15-minute Calibration Driving task followed by three counter-balanced 30-minute Mind Wandering driving conditions (A: task-relevant audio, B: non-task-relevant audio, C: internal-focus audio) in a driving simulator. EEG was recorded with BioSemi 64(+8) channel systems with 4 eye and 2 mastoid channels at 2048 Hz, along with SMI REDEYE250 eye tracking and vehicle logs at 100 Hz. Participants responded to lateral perturbations and pressed a button on detecting police vehicles.",
